# 55. DAY 36 PRACTICAL LAB

## Step 1 — Start Kali

Open a terminal.

Check your network configuration:

```bash
ip addr
```

Check routes:

```bash
ip route
```

---

## Step 2 — Identify Your Lab Target

Use your known intentionally vulnerable lab target.

For example:

```text
192.168.56.101
```

Do not substitute an arbitrary Internet target.

---

## Step 3 — Start Metasploit

```bash
msfconsole
```

---

## Step 4 — Search

Search for a service/vulnerability that exists on your lab target:

```text
search <service-or-vulnerability>
```

---

## Step 5 — Read Information

```text
info <module>
```

Review:

- Description
- References
- Options
- Targets
- Payloads

---

## Step 6 — Select the Module

```text
use <module>
```

---

## Step 7 — Review Options

```text
show options
```

Identify:

```text
RHOSTS
RPORT
```

and any module-specific required settings.

---

## Step 8 — Configure ONLY Your Lab

Example:

```text
set RHOSTS 192.168.56.101
```

If needed:

```text
set RPORT <lab-service-port>
```

---

## Step 9 — Review Payloads

```text
show payloads
```

Select only a compatible payload appropriate for your isolated lab.

---

## Step 10 — Check

If supported:

```text
check
```

Record the result.

---

## Step 11 — Controlled Exploitation

Only if:

- The target is yours/authorized
- The exploit is appropriate
- The lab is isolated
- The action is within your rules

Then execute the selected module.

---

## Step 12 — Observe Session

If successful:

```text
sessions
```

Document the session and stop when the exercise is complete.

---

## Step 13 — Reset Lab

Restore your snapshot or reset the vulnerable environment.

